# Shell scripts must keep LF endings regardless of the platform they are edited on.
#
# These are checked out on Linux and macOS -- the deploy runner, and whichever machine runs the
# migration. A CRLF checkout makes the shebang read as "/usr/bin/env bash\r", and the kernel
# reports "bad interpreter: no such file or directory", which is a confusing way to be told about
# a line ending. Authoring on Windows makes that the default without this rule.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the other things executed rather than read. YAML is whitespace-significant and
# CloudFormation and GitHub Actions both parse it on Linux.
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.bicep text eol=lf

# SQL migrations are applied by the server on Linux. LF keeps the checksums and diffs stable
# whichever platform wrote them.
*.sql text eol=lf
